    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it's basically just a physical hack to trick the brain into relaxing? i noticed that when i'm really anxious, it feels like there's this weird, restless energy or a 'buzzing' sensation in my chest that makes it hard to focus on the breathing. is there a biological reason for that part of it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That \"buzzing\" sensation occurs because anxiety triggers the release of adrenaline, which floods your bloodstream and causes rapid, involuntary muscle contractions in your chest and heart. This chemical surge forces your nervous system into a high-alert state, making the physical rigidity required for box breathing difficult to initiate. Over time, consistent practice can help downregulate these stress hormones, allowing your body to achieve the relaxation needed to override that restless feeling.",
      "source": "target",
